    "body": "### Describe the bug\n\nPassing a literal `None` as the `start` or `end` argument of optimized string methods produces incorrect results or compilation errors in Cython.\n\nThis works fine in Python, but it doesn’t work when the receiver is explicitly typed as `str` or `bytes` in Cython.\n\n### Code to reproduce the behaviour:\n\n**Example 1 (Incorrect result)**\n\n```cython\ndef f(str s):\n    return s.startswith(\"a\", None)\n```\n\n```python\nf(\"abc\")\n```\n\nPython returns True, whereas Cython returns False.\n\n**Example 2 (Compilation error)**\n\n```cython\ndef g(str s):\n    return s.find(\"b\", 0, None)\n```\n\nThis should return 1, but Cython fails to compile the generated C code with a \"too many arguments\" error.\n\n### Expected behaviour\n\nCython should treat a literal `None` in the `start` and `end` arguments the same as Python does, using the default boundaries.\n\nBoth examples should produce the same results as their Python equivalents.\n\n### OS\n\nWindows 10 (Build 10.0.26300, AMD64)\n\n### Python version\n\nCPython 3.11.14 (64-bit)\n\n### Cython version\n\n3.3.0\n\n### Additional context\n\n_No response_",
